# dist/ ships in the published tarball (see "files" in package.json), but
# the test files compiled into it alongside real source do not -- they're
# built into the same dist/ tree as everything else (see build.mjs's
# header comment: one esbuild pass over all of src/**/*.ts, tests included,
# so `node --test dist/**/*.test.js` exercises the literal shipped
# artifact). This excludes every compiled test-file variant (transpiled JS,
# its sourcemap, its declaration file, and the declaration's sourcemap) so
# none of them leak into what `npm publish`/`npm pack` actually ships.
#
# VERIFIED GAP (spot-checked in the final-cutover batch via a real
# `npm pack --dry-run`, as the migration plan flagged as unconfirmed): with
# this repo's npm (8.15.0), listing a bare directory ("dist") in package.json's
# "files" includes it WHOLESALE -- .npmignore has NO effect on paths inside
# it; a dry-run with only this file in place still packed every
# dist/**/*.test.* variant. The actual, verified-working exclusion is the
# negated glob entries in package.json's own "files" array
# (!dist/**/*.test.js etc.), which npm's own "files" matching (not a
# separate ignore file) does honor. This .npmignore is kept anyway, with
# the same patterns, as harmless defense-in-depth in case a different npm
# version's precedence between "files" and .npmignore differs -- but do
# not assume this file alone does the job; the "files" array is what's
# load-bearing.
dist/**/*.test.js
dist/**/*.test.js.map
dist/**/*.test.d.ts
dist/**/*.test.d.ts.map
